<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  CI-only build overrides for the driver build-VERIFICATION job.

  This file is imported ONLY in CI, via the documented MSBuild property
  /p:ForceImportAfterCppTargets=<path-to-this-file> (which Microsoft.Cpp.targets
  imports at the very end, after the WDK's own targets). The real VM / production
  driver build does NOT pass that property, so it is completely unaffected: it
  still stamps, verifies, and signs the INF and the .sys exactly as before.

  Why it exists
  -------------
  The GitHub-hosted windows-2022 runner ships a WDK (10.0.26100) whose InfVerif
  MSBuild task cannot load its own companion DLL:

      error : INF verification exception: Unable to load DLL 'x86\InfVerif.dll':
      The specified module could not be found. (HRESULT 0x8007007E)

  INF verification is a packaging/deployment check, not part of compiling and
  linking the kernel driver. The CI "Drivers (WDK build)" job exists only to
  prove that the driver C/C++ compiles and links clean as a real kernel-mode
  binary (toolset WindowsKernelModeDriver10.0, /kernel, KMDF). Loading, INF
  verification, catalog/signing, and runtime behaviour are verified in the
  disposable VM (see vm/VERIFICATION-RUNBOOK.md), never on a host and never in
  this build job.

  So for CI we replace the broken InfVerif step with a no-op. MSBuild honours
  the LAST definition of a target by name, and because this file is imported
  after the WDK targets, this empty InfVerif target wins and the broken tool is
  never invoked. (Driver signing is disabled separately and cleanly with
  /p:SignMode=Off on the msbuild command line, so the TestSign step does not run
  either — CI has no code-signing certificate; test-signing happens in the VM.)
-->
<Project xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <Target Name="InfVerif" />
</Project>
